name: parcel
after: 21-integrity
intro: You are about to hand these files to someone else, or to yourself on a
intro: worse day. Three programs about getting them there intact: pack them,
intro: prove two copies are the same, and leave a record that says so.
---
q: pack.sh -- box a directory up and count what went in
spec: It takes a directory and an archive name. It makes a gzip-compressed
spec: tar archive of that directory under that name, then prints how many
spec: entries the archive holds and nothing else.
spec:
spec: With anything other than exactly two arguments it exits 2.
file: pack.sh
prog: #!/bin/sh
prog: [ $# -eq 2 ] || exit 2
prog: tar -czf "$2" "$1" || exit 1
prog: tar -tzf "$2" | wc -l
case: sh pack.sh @dir@ out.tgz
case: sh pack.sh @dir2@ out.tgz; ls out.tgz | wc -l
case: sh pack.sh; echo $?
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the same tool that writes the archive can read its table of contents back without unpacking it
---
q: same.sh -- are these two files the same bytes
spec: It takes two files. It prints same and exits 0 when every byte matches,
spec: or differs and exits 1 when they do not.
spec:
spec: Nothing else reaches the screen either way, whatever the files are.
spec:
spec: With anything other than exactly two arguments it exits 2.
file: same.sh
prog: #!/bin/sh
prog: [ $# -eq 2 ] || exit 2
prog: if cmp -s "$1" "$2"; then echo same; else echo differs; exit 1; fi
case: sh same.sh @txt@ @txt@; echo $?
case: sh same.sh @txt@ @log@; echo $?
case: sh same.sh @txt@ @txt2@; echo $?
case: sh same.sh @txt@; echo $?
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the comparison tool already says yes or no in its exit status -- the flag you want is the one that stops it saying anything else
---
q: sums.sh -- write a record, then prove it still holds
spec: It takes no arguments. It writes a file called sums.txt holding a
spec: sha256 checksum line for every .log file in the current directory,
spec: then checks every file back against that record.
spec:
spec: It prints one number: how many of them still match.
file: sums.sh
prog: #!/bin/sh
prog: sha256sum *.log > sums.txt
prog: sha256sum -c sums.txt | grep -c ': OK'
case: sh sums.sh
case: sh sums.sh; wc -l < sums.txt
case: sh sums.sh; sh sums.sh
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the checksum tool both writes the record and reads it back -- the second pass says OK or FAILED per line, and you are counting one of those
